CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11 --unwind 5 --no-unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ assertion std::get<0>\(t\) == 1: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ assertion std::get<1>\(t\) == 2: SUCCESS$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
std::tuple basic operations in C++11 mode: std::get<0> and std::get<1> on a
two-element tuple of the same type.

The run-time std::get chain (std::get -> std::__get_helper ->
std::_Tuple_impl::_M_head -> std::_Head_base::_M_head) now reads each element
from the correct subobject: the base-subobject byte offset is computed over the
data-only layout, so the non-first _Head_base of the recursive _Tuple_impl is
placed at its true offset and the constructor stores / get reads it there.  The
desc requires both per-element assertions to be reported SUCCESS so the pass is
non-vacuous.
